<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wei Qing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 106 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wei Qing was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty</a> general of the second century BCE who led major campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>. With his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a>, he conducted the decisive northern campaigns that drove Xiongnu power beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi Desert</a>. The Han Dynasty and Xiongnu Empire profiles treat these campaigns as a turning point in the long struggle between the two powers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wei Qing was born Zheng Qing, the illegitimate son of Zheng Ji, a low-ranking official serving at the estate of Princess Pingyang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>'s elder sister, and of a servant woman known as Wei Ao. Mistreated in his father's household and made to herd sheep, he ran back to his mother in his early teens, served as a stableboy on the estate and took her family's surname.</p><p>When Princess Pingyang gave his half-sister, the singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-zifu">Wei Zifu</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> around 139 BCE, he went with her and became a horseman of the royal guard. Grand Princess Guantao, mother of Empress Chen Jiao, had him kidnapped to be killed, but palace guards led by Gongsun Ao rescued him, and the emperor gave him high posts in response.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 129 BCE, as General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariots</a> and Cavalry, he led one of four columns of 10,000 cavalry against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>; while the others failed, he raided their holy site Longcheng, the first proper Han victory over them. In 127 BCE he outflanked the princes of Loufan and Baiyang, took over a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and drove them from the Hetao region, where the city of Shuofang was built; he was made Marquess Changping. In 124 BCE a night assault from Gaoque surprised the Right Worthy Prince's camp and took about 15,000 captives, and Wei Qing became Grand General.</p><p>In the Mobei campaign of 119 BCE he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> each led 50,000 cavalry and 100,000 infantry and charioteers across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobi-desert">Gobi</a>. Meeting the main army of Chanyu Yizhixie, Wei Qing held its charges with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> arranged in rings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> until a sandstorm at dusk let him attack both flanks, routing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and pursuing them to the region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a>. The flanking force of Li Guang and Zhao Yiji lost its way, and Li Guang killed himself rather than face a court martial; the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, among others, blamed Wei Qing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After 119 BCE Wei Qing saw little fighting and stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> as Grand Master of the Horse and Grand General, advising on military matters. He kept a low profile and refused to hire scholars to praise him. He died in 106 BCE and was buried in a tomb modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Mount Lu</a>, joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a>'s and to the emperor's own. He did not see the destruction of his clan in the turmoil of 91 BCE, in which his sister Empress Wei and Liu Ju died and his son Wei Kang was executed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wei Qing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>General <b>Wei Qing</b> and the cavalry commander Huo Qubing conducted the decisive northern campaigns, which ended in 119 BCE and drove Xiongnu power beyond the Gobi Desert.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The expeditions of the generals <b>Wei Qing</b> and Huo Qubing between 129 and 119 BCE seized the Hexi Corridor in 121 BCE and ended with the Battle of Mobei north of the Gobi in 119 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Wu of Han broke with it in 133 BCE, and campaigns under <b>Wei Qing</b> and Huo Qubing drove the chanyu’s court north of the Gobi in 119 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Wei Qing</b>, the son of a servant woman in a princess’s household, worked as a sheepherder and stableboy before his half-sister Wei Zifu became Emperor Wu’s empress; he rose to be Grand General and led the armies against the Xiongnu.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yaodu District, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.08,111.51&amp;year=-116" aria-label="Yaodu District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wei Qing"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4416 4459h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4416 4459h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.08,111.51&amp;year=-116">What was here in 116 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
